.TH PPV 1 "2026-11-07" "ppv 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ppv \- portable pipe viewer: show the throughput of a pipe or a process
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cmd |
.B ppv
.RI [ OPTION ]...
.B | cmd
.br
.B ppv
.B \-\-pid
.I PID
.RI [ OPTION ]...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ppv
copies standard input to standard output and shows on standard error how fast
the data flows. With
.B \-\-pid
it copies nothing and instead shows the read and write throughput of a running
process.
.PP
There is no progress bar and no estimated time:
.B ppv
cannot know the size of a stream and does not pretend to.
.PP
The live line is updated once per
.I interval
(default 1 second) and redrawn in place with a carriage return. It is only
shown when standard error is a terminal (see
.BR \-f ).
.PP
.EX
[105M, 12.3M/s, 45.6M/s, 98.7M/s]
.EE
.PP
is the total so far, then the minimum, average and maximum rate over the last
minute. With
.B \-\-pid
the line shows both directions:
.PP
.EX
[R 0B, 0B/s, 0B/s, 0B/s | W 3.84G, 1.06G/s, 1.11G/s, 1.14G/s]
.EE
.PP
Sizes use binary units (K = 1024) with at most three significant digits.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-stats
Print statistics on standard error when the transfer ends (see
.BR STATISTICS ).
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Do not show the live line. Takes precedence over
.BR \-f .
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-force
Show the live line even if standard error is not a terminal.
.TP
.BR \-W ", " \-\-wait
Show nothing until the first byte has arrived (with
.BR \-\-pid :
until the process has read or written something).
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-interval " " \fISEC\fR
Update interval in seconds, 0.1 to 60, as a plain decimal number
(default 1). The interval is also the sampling period of the statistics.
.TP
.BR \-N ", " \-\-name " " \fINAME\fR
Prefix the live line and the statistics with
.IR NAME
(at most 128 characters), useful when several
.B ppv
run in one pipeline. An empty name is ignored.
.TP
.BR \-B ", " \-\-buffer\-size " " \fISIZE\fR
Size of the transfer buffer, 4K to 256M, with an optional suffix
.BR K ", " M " or " G
(default 1M).
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-pid " " \fIPID\fR
Do not copy anything; watch the I/O counters of process
.I PID
instead and stop when it exits. See
.BR "WATCHING A PROCESS" .
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show the usage and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Show the version and exit.
.PP
Short options can be bundled
.RB ( \-sq )
and take their argument attached or separate
.RB ( \-i0.5 ", " "\-i 0.5" ).
Long options take it with
.B =
or separate.
.B ppv
accepts no file arguments: it always reads standard input.
.SH STATISTICS
With
.B \-s
the following is printed when the transfer ends:
.PP
.EX
ppv: 20.0M (20971520 bytes) in 3.1s, avg 6.51M/s
  rate     min 0B/s, p50 0B/s, p90 62.0K/s, max 64.0K/s (3 x 1.0s)
  waiting  input 0.0s (1%), output 3.0s (99%): the consumer (output side) is the bottleneck
  io       389 transfers, avg 52.6K, splice
.EE
.TP
.B rate
Minimum, median, 90th percentile and maximum of the per-interval rates, and
how many intervals were measured. The percentiles come from a logarithmic
histogram with about 6% resolution.
.TP
.B waiting
How long
.B ppv
was blocked reading (the producer was slower) and writing (the consumer was
slower), as time and as share of the run time. One side is named as the
bottleneck when it accounts for at least half of the run time and more than
the other side.
.TP
.B io
Number of read/write cycles, their average size, and the transfer method:
.B splice
(Linux, zero copy),
.B read/write
(other POSIX systems, or Linux when a side cannot be spliced, e.g. a terminal
or a file opened for appending) or
.B ReadFile/WriteFile
(Windows).
.PP
After an interruption the first line ends with
.BR "(interrupted)" .
.SH WATCHING A PROCESS
.B ppv \-\-pid
.I PID
reads the I/O counters of another process once per interval:
.IR /proc/PID/io
.RB ( rchar ", " wchar )
on Linux,
.B GetProcessIoCounters
on Windows. The counters include all I/O of the process: files, pipes and
sockets. Watching ends when the process exits, or with Ctrl-C.
.PP
Linux needs a kernel with I/O accounting
.RB ( CONFIG_TASK_IO_ACCOUNTING ,
standard on distribution kernels, missing on WSL2) and permission to read the
process (same user, or root). The last partial interval before the process
exits is lost, because
.I /proc
is gone by then; Windows still delivers the final counters. Other POSIX
systems are not supported.
.PP
In git-bash and MSYS2 the shell's
.B $!
is an MSYS PID;
.B ppv
needs the Windows PID
.RB ( "ps \-W" ,
.BR "cat /proc/PID/winpid" ).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The input ended and everything was written, or the watched process exited.
.TP
.B 1
An error occurred, or the reader of standard output went away before the input
ended (the message is
.BR "output closed before the input ended" ).
.TP
.B 2
Usage error.
.TP
.B 130
Interrupted with Ctrl-C (SIGINT).
.TP
.B 143
Terminated with SIGTERM.
.PP
On POSIX systems
.B ppv
ends by re-raising the signal after printing the statistics, so the shell sees
a death by signal, as with any other filter.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
None. The locale is not consulted; numbers are always printed with a decimal
point.
.SH NOTES
Standard input and output are used as binary streams on all platforms; on
Windows no CR/LF translation takes place. Standard descriptors that are closed
at start-up are reported as an error instead of being reused.
.PP
The live line never reaches the last column of the terminal and therefore
never wraps; it is cut if the terminal is narrower.
.PP
Only SIGINT and SIGTERM are handled. SIGPIPE is ignored so that a closed
output is reported with exit status 1.
.SH EXAMPLES
Copy a backup over the network and see where the time goes:
.PP
.EX
tar cf \- data | ppv \-s | ssh host "cat > backup.tar"
.EE
.PP
Two meters in one pipeline, named:
.PP
.EX
cat big.iso | ppv \-N raw | gzip | ppv \-N gz > big.iso.gz
.EE
.PP
Watch what a running process reads and writes, two updates per second:
.PP
.EX
ppv \-\-pid 1234 \-i 0.5
.EE
.PP
Quiet copy with statistics only, for a script:
.PP
.EX
ppv \-q \-s < input > output 2> stats.txt
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pv (1),
.BR dd (1),
.BR splice (2),
.BR proc (5)
.PP
